{"chapter": "spaces-topologies", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "03Y5", "placeholders": {"REF_0001": "\\cite{SGA1}", "REF_0002": "\\cite{Ner}", "REF_0003": "\\cite{LM-B}", "REF_0004": "\\cite{Kn}", "REF_0005": "\\ref{sites-definition-site}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:ea0f4c0080e9b15b740891acfe2fd4779d43d916df52cda08464d7c0dff8502e", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nIn this chapter we introduce some topologies on the\ncategory of algebraic spaces. Compare with the material in <REF_0001>,\n<REF_0002>, <REF_0003> and <REF_0004>.\nBefore doing so we would like to point out that there\nare many different choices of sites (as defined in\nSites, Definition <REF_0005>) which give rise to\nthe same notion of sheaf on the underlying category. Hence\nour choices may be slightly different from those in the references\nbut ultimately lead to the same cohomology groups, etc.\n\n\n\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:54a2625f6081674d48b3adbb0113b44155e33346de6dd128961f2ebda75b4fc3", "unit_id": "tag:03Y5:prose-0001"}
